# Training materials: 6th grade, image rich, original (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-07): **training materials MUST be written
for readers with at most a 6th grade education and no knowledge of IT or
Security, and must be image rich to enforce lessons. Do not use copyrighted
materials — use only open source or create original documentation.**

Thoropass (SOC 2) and HR platforms like ADP and Paychex ship the same
assign-read-attest workflow, and they win on **comprehension**, not on
control-mapping density. A workforce member who cannot read the document
cannot be trained by it, and an attestation collected against text the
reader did not understand is a signature on nothing — audit theater that
fails the moment an assessor interviews the workforce.

## The rule

> **Every training or awareness document this product ships or renders is
> written at a Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level of 6.0 or lower, assumes zero IT
> or security background, carries at least one original illustration per
> major lesson with real alternative text, and contains no third-party
> copyrighted content. Original prose and original diagrams, or
> public-domain / permissively-licensed source with the license recorded.
> Dense professional prose is a defect, not a style choice.**

## 1. Reading level — measured, not asserted

| Requirement | Threshold | Enforced by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level | **<= 6.0** per document | `training::readability::grade_level` |
| Longest single sentence | **<= 25 words** | `training::readability::long_sentences` |
| Unexplained IT / security jargon | **zero** | `training::readability::banned_jargon` |

Flesch-Kincaid is the formula the US Government already applies to itself
(Plain Writing Act of 2010 guidance; DoDI 5025.13), so "how do you know
this is 6th-grade text?" has a checkable answer.

**Averages hide monsters.** A document can average grade 5 and still
contain one 60-word sentence that stops every reader. Both the average
and the worst sentence are checked.

### Writing moves that get you there

| Instead of | Write |
| --- | --- |
| "Personnel must authenticate prior to accessing the enclave." | "Prove who you are before you open the work area." |
| "Exfiltration of CUI is prohibited." | "Never move these files out of the work area." |
| "Report suspected incidents to the ISSO without delay." | "Think something went wrong? Tell {security_contact_name} right away." |
| "Utilize multifactor authentication." | "Use your password and one more step, like a code on your phone." |
| "Notwithstanding the foregoing, deviations require approval." | "You can only skip this if your manager says yes in writing." |

One idea per sentence. One lesson per section. Second person ("you"),
active voice, present tense. Define a term the first time you need it,
then use it freely. Numbers as digits ("72 hours"). Never make the reader
hold two ideas to understand a third.

## 2. Jargon — define on first use or do not use it

Flesch-Kincaid cannot see jargon. "Phishing" is two syllables and scores
as easy text, but a reader with no security background does not know it.
The term list in `training::readability::banned_jargon` pairs each term
with a plain replacement. Using the term is allowed **after** the document
has explained it in plain words.

Forbidden outright, with no definition-and-proceed path, because a plain
word already exists: `utilize` (use), `commence` (start), `terminate`
(end), `ascertain` (find out), `prior to` (before), `subsequent` (later),
`in the event that` (if), `pursuant to` (under), `notwithstanding` (even
so), `disseminate` (share), `promulgate` (publish).

## 3. Image rich — illustrations carry the lesson

> **At least one illustration per major lesson.** A wall of text is a
> failed training document even when it scores grade 4.

| Requirement | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Coverage | Every document has **>= 1** illustration; documents over 6 sections have **>= 3** |
| Source | **Original** SVG authored in this workspace, or public-domain / permissive-license art with the license recorded next to it |
| Alt text | **Required, non-empty, and load-bearing** — it must teach the same lesson in words (Section 508 / WCAG 1.1.1) |
| Caption | Required. States the lesson, not "Figure 3" |
| Plaintext fallback | Alt text renders in `plaintext` and `markdown` exports so no format loses the lesson |
| Contrast / color | WCAG 2.0 AA; never convey meaning by color alone (a red X and a green check must also differ in shape) |
| Text in image | Short labels only. Never put a sentence a reader needs *only* inside an image |

Styles that work for this audience: do-this / not-this pairs, numbered
step strips, simple boundary diagrams (what is inside the protected area
and what is outside), and traffic-light decision trees.

Training illustrations are **document content**, not product branding, so
they live with the documents rather than in `core-assets`. Icons, logos,
and header art remain governed by
[`branding-assets.mdc`](branding-assets.mdc) and must still come from
`core-assets`. Never ship a noise / placeholder bitmap
([`no-noise-desktop-icons.mdc`](no-noise-desktop-icons.mdc)).

## 4. Copyright — original or open source only

> **Never paste third-party copyrighted training content into this
> workspace.** Not vendor courseware, not another compliance platform's
> document library, not stock illustrations under a non-commercial or
> unclear license, not a chapter of a book, not a competitor's SOC 2
> awareness deck.

| Source | Status | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Original prose written for this product | **preferred** | Default for every document |
| Original SVG diagrams authored here | **preferred** | Default for every illustration |
| US Government works (NIST SP, CISA, NARA CUI Registry, 32 CFR, DFARS, FAR) | allowed | 17 U.S.C. 105 — not copyrightable. Cite the publication |
| Public domain (CC0) / permissive (CC-BY with attribution recorded) | allowed | Record license and attribution beside the asset |
| Vendor / competitor courseware, paid stock, books, blogs | **forbidden** | Includes "lightly reworded" derivatives |

Quoting a **regulation** is fine and expected — that is the authority the
document exists to explain. Quoting somebody's **explanation** of the
regulation is not. Citing a clause is not the same as claiming compliance
([`compliance-claims-honesty.mdc`](compliance-claims-honesty.mdc)).

## 5. Forbidden constructs

- A shipping document scoring above grade 6.0.
- Any sentence over 25 words.
- Jargon from the banned list used before it is explained.
- A document with zero illustrations, or an illustration with empty,
  decorative, or duplicate-of-caption alt text.
- An illustration that carries a required lesson with no text equivalent.
- Third-party copyrighted prose or art, including reworded derivatives.
- Lowering `MAX_GRADE_LEVEL`, weakening the enforcing test, or adding an
  allow-list of "legacy" documents to make a failing catalog pass.
  **Rewrite the document.**
- Claiming a document is 6th-grade without the measured score.

## 6. Required agent behavior

1. When authoring or editing any training document, run the readability
   audit and report the measured grade for every document touched:
   `cargo test -p pim-offline-server --lib training::readability -- --nocapture`
2. Land illustrations **in the same change set** as the prose. "Text now,
   pictures later" ships the defect this rule exists to prevent.
3. Never weaken the threshold to make a document pass.
4. Attestation surfaces inherit this rule: the consent text a person
   signs, the reminder email, and the completion screen are all training
   materials.
5. Mirror this rule to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change set
   ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)) — the workspace
   `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation** (the style shipped before this rule, grade 15.9): one
34-word sentence, five words over four syllables, no illustration, and it
never says what the reader should do.

```text
Organizations must implement configuration management processes that ensure
the authorized baseline is maintained throughout the system development
lifecycle, including the documentation of deviations and the subsequent
remediation of identified nonconformities.
```

**Fix** (grade 4.4, one lesson, one picture):

```text
## Do not change the setup on your own

Your computer is set up a certain way on purpose. Someone checked that the
setup is safe.

If you change it, the check no longer counts.

Need something changed? Ask {security_contact_name} first. Wait for a yes.
Write down what changed and when.

[Illustration: two laptops side by side. The left one has a green check and
the label "Setup approved". The right one has a red X, a new program being
installed, and the label "Changed without asking".
Alt text: An approved computer marked with a green check next to a changed
computer marked with a red X, showing that installing software without
approval breaks the safety check.
Caption: Ask before you change anything on your work computer.]
```

